/-! # Cartier boundary of a fibrewise dense affine open

Source: Guo, Divisors on coherent schemes and homogeneous spaces, Proposition 3.3,
(iii) implies (i) and (ii), especially the Noetherian argument using EGA IV4,
Proposition 21.14.3 (blueprint: lem-sfb-smooth-normal-affine-boundary-cartier).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

/-- The reduced boundary of an affine open dense in every fibre of a smooth separated
finitely presented morphism over an affine locally Noetherian normal base is Cartier.
`Smooth f` supplies local finite presentation and `IsSeparated f` supplies
quasi-separatedness, so `QuasiCompact f` supplies the remaining finite-presentation condition.
The reduced boundary is the subscheme of `vanishingIdeal U.compl`, and `IsInvertible`
means that this ideal is locally generated by one non-zero-divisor.

Source: Guo, Divisors on coherent schemes and homogeneous spaces, Proposition 3.3,
(iii) implies (i) and (ii), and its final Noetherian proof paragraph; EGA IV4,
Proposition 21.14.3 (blueprint: lem-sfb-smooth-normal-affine-boundary-cartier).

Proof:
1. The affine locally Noetherian base is Noetherian. Finite presentation gives finite
   type, so X is Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian. By
   lem-sc-separated-over-affine-scheme-separated, X is separated. Base change
   preserves finite presentation (lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence), so each
   residue-field fibre is also Noetherian.
2. Fibrewise density implies density of U in X: a nonempty open meets some fibre,
   and its intersection with that fibre meets U. The closed complement has finitely
   many irreducible components (lem-sc-noetherian-closed-subset-finite-components).
   At each generic point their local rings have dimension one by
   lem-sfb-affine-open-boundary-codimension-one.
3. No boundary component contains a fibre component. Each fibre has finitely many
   irreducible components (lem-sc-noetherian-closed-subset-finite-components).
   The complement of all components other than one is a nonempty open within that
   component. It must meet the dense inverse image of U, excluding its containment
   in the boundary. Empty fibres impose no condition.
4. Give each boundary component its reduced structure using
   lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme. Apply
   lem-sfb-ramanujam-samuel-prime-divisor: the base is normal locally Noetherian,
   the morphism is smooth, each component has codimension one, and step 3 excludes
   every fibre component. Each reduced component ideal has a non-zero-divisor generator.
5. By the pointwise vanishing description in
   lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme, the reduced boundary ideal is the
   finite intersection of these component ideals. At a point, discard components
   missing that point. The remaining stalk ideals are distinct incomparable principal
   primes (a_i); localization preserves their distinctness and incomparability.
   Their intersection equals the ideal generated by the product of the a_i:
   if a_1 c lies in another prime (a_j), then a_1 does not lie there, hence c does.
   Induction proves the formula. Finite intersections commute with stalks, by choosing
   a common neighbourhood for finitely many germs. Shrink so that all component
   generators are defined and generate their ideals. The same stalk calculation
   identifies their product with the reduced boundary ideal on this neighbourhood.
   The product is a non-zero-divisor since every factor is; this gives
   def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal. The reduced-subscheme theorem gives its image.
   For the empty boundary the intersection is the unit ideal and the product is 1;
   thus U = X and the empty scheme are included. -/
theorem isInvertible_vanishingIdeal_compl_of_smooth_fiberwise_dense_affine
    {T X : Scheme.{u}} [IsAffine T] [IsLocallyNoetherian T]
    (hT : ∀ t : T, IsDomain (T.presheaf.stalk t) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (T.presheaf.stalk t))
    (f : X ⟶ T) [Smooth f] [IsSeparated f] [QuasiCompact f]
    (U : X.Opens) (hU : IsAffineOpen U)
    (hfib : ∀ t : T, Dense ((f.fiberι t) ⁻¹' (U : Set X))) :
    (vanishingIdeal U.compl).IsInvertible ∧
      Set.range (vanishingIdeal U.compl).subschemeι = (U : Set X)ᶜ := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData
